.TH SYS_FUTEX 2
.SH NAME
sys_futex \- sleep on a memory word, wake its sleepers (SYS_FUTEX)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_futex(int op, volatile void *addr, long val, uint64_t ns);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The base of locks and condition variables that cost nothing while
uncontended.
.TP
.B FUTEX_WAIT
If the 32-bit word at
.I addr
still holds
.IR val ,
sleep until woken, at most
.I ns
nanoseconds (0: no limit). If it does not, return
.B EAGAIN
at once: the check and the sleep are one step, so a wake-up between them
is never lost.
.TP
.B FUTEX_WAKE
Wake up to
.I val
threads sleeping on the word at
.IR addr .
.PP
Futexes are keyed by the word's memory, not its address, so they also
work between processes that share it
.RB ( sys_shm_create (2)).
.I addr
must be 4-byte aligned.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR FUTEX_WAIT :
0 when woken.
.BR FUTEX_WAKE :
how many threads were woken. Otherwise a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EAGAIN
The word does not hold
.IR val .
.TP
.B ETIMEDOUT
The time limit passed.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The process is ending.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I addr
is not aligned, or not the caller's memory.
.TP
.B EINVAL
An unknown
.IR op .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_thread_self (2),
.BR sys_note (2),
.BR sys_mmap (2),
.BR sys_sleep (2)
